+# Veterinarian Free-Text Search Stays a Non-Goal, Specialty Filtering Does Not
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+> **Decided 2026-08-07 by the product owner.** Unlike the 2026-07-31 entries in this log, this decision was made and recorded on the same day, so every section below is stated intent rather than reconstruction.
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined search for pets, visits, and veterinarians on one reason: owner search already demonstrates paged search once, and repeating the pattern adds surface without adding understanding. A request then arrived to narrow the veterinarian directory to a single specialty.
+
+That request sits on the boundary NG-9 drew. It asks for less than a search — no free text, no new entry point, no second search pattern — but the existing wording covers it, so the boundary had to be redrawn deliberately rather than read around.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Hold NG-9 as written** — decline the request. Keeps the non-goal table untouched, and keeps the directory a plain published list.
+2. **Withdraw NG-9** — make every entity searchable. Reverses the reason NG-9 records and multiplies the search surface across pets, visits, and veterinarians.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** — keep free-text search for pets, visits, and veterinarians out, and admit narrowing a published list by an attribute that list already shows.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. NG-9 now declines *free-text* search for pets, visits, and veterinarians, and admits narrowing the veterinarian directory to one specialty. The narrowing is stated as [`REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004).
+
+The line is drawn at what the reader supplies. Free text against an unbounded field is a search pattern the owner capability already teaches. Choosing one value from a small, bounded set the page already prints beside every veterinarian is not — it reuses the published list rather than adding a search surface to it.
+
+The same conversation reinstated the machine-readable veterinarian list as a supported surface, recorded as [`REQ-VET-003`](../prd.md#req-vet-003). `REQ-VET-002`, which described that surface before it was withdrawn on 2026-07-31, stays withdrawn and its ID is never reused.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9's reason survives intact for the cases it still covers: pets and visits remain unsearchable, and there is still no free-text lookup of a veterinarian by name.
+- The veterinarian directory gains a second reason to exist beyond G-1's "read one capability end to end" — it now demonstrates narrowing a published collection, which nothing else in the sample shows.
+- The bounded-set test is the reusable form of this decision. A future request to filter by a bounded, already-displayed attribute has a precedent; a request for free text over an open field meets NG-9 unchanged.
+- Reinstating a withdrawn surface establishes that withdrawal is reversible in this project. The Superseded list therefore records amendments rather than being append-only fact.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9 — narrowed, not withdrawn. The admitted scope is stated in [`REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004); the reinstated surface in [`REQ-VET-003`](../prd.md#req-vet-003).
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md § Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the amended NG-9 row
+- [prd.md § Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#veterinarian-directory) — `REQ-VET-003` and `REQ-VET-004`
+- [prd.md § Superseded](../prd.md#superseded) — why `REQ-VET-002` stays withdrawn
